{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'git diff --no-ext-diff --unified=80 master -- docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md public/chp/blog/yin-vm-vs-unison.blog'","aggregated_output":"2026-09-23 23:57:38.390 xcodebuild[31237:21750166]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-09-23 23:57:38.559 xcodebuild[31237:21750165] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ngit: warning: confstr() failed with code 5: couldn't get path of DARWIN_USER_TEMP_DIR; using /tmp instead\ngit: error: couldn't create cache file '/tmp/xcrun_db-4MZA3OAB' (errno=Operation not permitted)\n2026-09-23 23:57:39.119 xcodebuild[31241:21750184]  DVTFilePathFSEvents: Failed to start fs event stream.\n2026-09-23 23:57:39.290 xcodebuild[31241:21750182] [MT] DVTDeveloperPaths: Failed to get length of DARWIN_USER_CACHE_DIR from confstr(3), error = Error Domain=NSPOSIXErrorDomain Code=5 \"Input/output error\". Using NSCachesDirectory instead.\ndiff --git a/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md b/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md\nindex e0e81645..ca66820f 100644\n--- a/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md\n+++ b/docs/design/yin.vm.debruijn-projection.md\n@@ -81,260 +81,269 @@ The walked node grammar and child order are:\n | `:vm/park` | none | none |\n | `:vm/resume` | `:yin/parked-id`, `:yin/val-node` | value |\n | `:stream/make` | `:yin/buffer` | none |\n | `:stream/put` | `:yin/target`, `:yin/val-node` | target, value |\n | `:stream/cursor`, `:stream/next`, `:stream/close` | source | source |\n \n The table is exactly the emitter's vocabulary. `:yin/tail?` is derived\n control-position data: it is ignored, excluded from rows and hashes, and is\n not persisted by this projection.\n \n The walk follows fixed child order. A vector is ordered data, never a set. A\n shared source entity is memoized by `[source-eid lexical-context]`, where\n `lexical-context` is the complete stack of frame parameter vectors. This memo\n is only an optimisation; it must never affect output identity. A finite AST\n graph is required; cycles are rejected.\n \n ## 3. Scope and de Bruijn resolution\n \n The scope stack contains ordered parameter vectors. Frame depth `0` is the\n innermost frame; position is the source parameter index, with the leftmost\n parameter at position `0`. Entering a lambda pushes one frame and leaving its\n body pops it. The lambda's names are otherwise discarded; its binder becomes\n an arity.\n \n Resolution searches frames from inner to outer. Within one frame it searches\n positions right-to-left. Thus duplicate parameters have rightmost-wins\n semantics, matching VM `bind-params`: `(fn [x x] x)` resolves to the second\n `x`. A bound occurrence is represented as `{:bound [frame-depth position]}`.\n An occurrence with no matching binder is `{:free name}` and preserves its\n symbol exactly. An intermediate binder shifts only its own frame depth; the\n position pair does not depend on the arity of intervening frames.\n \n Every child is projected under the scope at its occurrence. A lambda body is\n projected after its frame is pushed; an application operator and operands share\n the enclosing scope.\n \n ## 4. Projected form and published dimension\n \n The projection defines and exports a `:yin.debruijn/*` dimension descriptor,\n whose content hash is the node-hash domain separator. Persistent publication or\n discovery of that descriptor bundle is outside D0–D6; no external registry is\n required by the dimension protocol. A projected semantic node is a\n content-addressed record whose identity is a 32-byte node hash `h`.\n \n The descriptor declares the projected d5 semantic slots in this order:\n `[:yin.debruijn/hash :yin.debruijn/type :yin.debruijn/arity\n :yin.debruijn/bound :yin.debruijn/free :yin.debruijn/value\n :yin.debruijn/op :yin.debruijn/key :yin.debruijn/prefix\n :yin.debruijn/buffer :yin.debruijn/parked-id :yin.debruijn/macro?\n :yin.debruijn/body :yin.debruijn/operator\n :yin.debruijn/operands :yin.debruijn/test :yin.debruijn/consequent\n :yin.debruijn/alternate :yin.debruijn/target :yin.debruijn/val-node\n :yin.debruijn/source :yin.debruijn/root]`. The descriptor also declares each\n slot's type and the ordered-vector type of `:yin.debruijn/operands`; its hash,\n not an ad hoc text string, separates this dimension from every other tuple\n dimension.\n \n The semantic tuple shape is:\n \n ```\n [h tag scalar-slots child-hashes-in-declared-order]\n ```\n \n The d5 storage adapter may use local entity `e` handles, but emits\n `:yin.debruijn/hash h` on each entity. Local eids, ordinals, row count, root\n ordinal, and the old `-16` base are storage layout only. They are never\n identity. Hash-consing equal subterms is the compression mechanism.\n \n Projected d5 attributes are:\n \n * `:yin.debruijn/type`, `:arity`, `:bound`, and `:free`;\n * scalar `:value`, `:op`, `:key`, `:prefix`, `:buffer`, `:parked-id`, and\n   `:macro?`;\n * child refs `:body`, `:operator`, `:operands`, `:test`, `:consequent`,\n   `:alternate`, `:target`, `:val-node`, and `:source`.\n \n Child refs contain child hashes semantically. `:operands` is an ordered vector\n of hashes and is never emitted as cardinality-many datoms. The root is an\n explicit root-hash marker. The named `:yin/name` and `:yin/params` attributes,\n `:yin/macro-name`, source eids, `:yin/tail?`, and transaction metadata are not\n-projected into identity. A source-to-projected map is ephemeral by default and, if persisted\n-for diagnostics, is a separate side index.\n+projected into identity. A source-to-projected map is ephemeral by default\n+and, if persisted for diagnostics, is a separate side index.\n \n ## 5. Merkle fingerprint and canonical encoding\n \n For every projected node:\n \n ```\n hash(node) = SHA-256(dimension-hash\n                      || encode(tag-specific-slots-in-descriptor-order))\n fingerprint = hash(root)\n ```\n \n The dimension hash follows the d1 content-addressing floor; an ad hoc string\n domain separator is not used. Only slots belonging to the node's tag are\n encoded, in descriptor order. `:yin.debruijn/hash` and\n `:yin.debruijn/root` are storage/index markers and are excluded from every\n node hash. Ordered child hashes preserve operand and branch order. Tree\n emission and graph emission of the same unfolded term therefore have the same\n root hash; memoisation and sharing cannot change identity.\n \n Every slot is tagged and length-delimited. Keywords and symbols encode\n namespace and name separately. Strings use UTF-8 NFC with length prefixes;\n bytes are length-prefixed; booleans are `00`/`01`; nil has its own tag; maps\n sort by canonical encoded key bytes; sets sort by canonical encoded element\n bytes. Lists and vectors are distinct sequence classes and never merge: the\n language observes the difference, so identity must not. Unsupported values\n are diagnostics, including an unpaired surrogate and a record literal; a map\n whose entries collide under key canonicalization is likewise a diagnostic,\n so iteration order never decides a hash. The preimage is a text stream: each\n part frames as its class's tag byte, an 8-hex-digit length, and the content —\n numeric and byte content is hex, string content is the raw UTF-8 text, and\n the length counts the content's UTF-8 bytes. The hashed bytes are the\n stream's UTF-8 encoding.\n \n-Numeric canonicalisation is fixed in D0: exact integers in signed int64 use\n-int64 encoding, and every integral double in that range also uses int64\n-encoding on every host. All other in-domain numbers use IEEE-754 doubles; all\n-NaNs use one quiet-NaN encoding and `+0` and `-0` remain distinct. A\n-JavaScript number is an int64 only when it is a safe integer in the int64 range;\n-an unsafe integer whose exact classification is not recoverable is diagnostic.\n-Consequently, integer `1` and integral double `1.0` intentionally collide in\n-the canonical form. Bigints, ratios, characters, and other out-of-domain\n-numeric objects are diagnostic. NFC normalisation collisions, and the\n-`1`/`1.0` collision, are inherited limits of the repository encoding.\n+Numeric canonicalisation (Contract Version 2, `:yin.debruijn/contract-version`\n+2, `:integral-double-folding false`): the classes `:int64` and `:double` are\n+disjoint. Exact integers in signed int64 use int64 encoding; every\n+floating-point value, integral or not, uses IEEE-754 double encoding and is\n+never folded into `:int64`. All NaNs use one quiet-NaN encoding and `+0` and\n+`-0` remain distinct. Consequently integer `1` and double `1.0` project to\n+distinct records and fingerprints, and `{1 :a, 1.0 :b}` and `#{1 1.0}` do not\n+collide. Version 1 folded integral doubles into int64; that is withdrawn.\n+\n+The boundary: JavaScript has one number type, so a JS host cannot tell `1`\n+from `1.0`. That is a host quirk, and it stays in the JS adapter: on CLJS a\n+safe integer classifies as `:int64`, a non-integral number as `:double`, and\n+an unsafe integral is diagnostic. It does not contaminate the universal\n+projection, whose bytes are type-preserving on JVM and Dart. A foreign record\n+claiming a class the host cannot represent is refused at the boundary with\n+`:unsupported-value`. Bigints, ratios, characters, and other out-of-domain\n+numeric objects are diagnostic. NFC normalisation collisions are an inherited\n+limit of the repository encoding.\n The Dart NFC source is settled 2026-09-21: the pure-Dart `unorm_dart`\n package (Unicode 16.0) provides NFC on every ClojureDart target. D3 still\n proves byte identity on all three hosts against the §8 fixtures.\n \n ## 6. Stream composition and consumers\n \n Implementation belongs in `src/cljc/yin/vm/debruijn.cljc`: it consumes the\n existing `:yin/*` schema and shares no transport code. It exposes:\n \n * `project-datoms`: pure projection of one complete rooted graph to Merkle\n   records and root fingerprint;\n * `forward-step`: ordinary `dao.stream` forward interpretation. It frames at\n   the root marker, projects there, emits the projected tuples, and reports a\n   partial frame at end as diagnostic. Pending writes remain explicit and are\n   retried only by host cadence.\n \n Named storage, renderers, Datalog queries, source maps, and diagnostics consume\n the named stream. Deduplication, cache lookup, and alpha-equivalence consume\n the projected segment/fingerprint. The named AST remains the execution source:\n `yin.vm/ast->datoms` emits named AST datoms; `yin.vm.linearize` lowers those\n datoms to `:yin.code/*` datoms, which the semantic VM loads and executes;\n `yin.vm.ast-walker` can evaluate the named AST map directly. None of these\n execution paths consumes the de Bruijn projection. A waitset is optional only\n for a composition that owns several independent waiters; no lease fact, lease\n timer, callback, or scheduler is introduced here.\n \n ## 7. Implementation plan\n \n ### D0 — contract, dimension, and fixtures\n \n Publish the `:yin.debruijn/*` descriptor and hash domain; record the fully\n expanded macro domain and its lambda-operator detection rule and limit,\n assert-only input, root framing and per-frame index reset, rightmost duplicate\n binding, scope pair convention, canonical value table, NFC and numeric limits,\n and node grammar. Pin the Dart NFC source (`unorm_dart`, Unicode 16.0)\n behind one host-dispatched normalize seam. Add all node fixtures.\n \n ### D1 — graph index and scope resolver\n \n Implement root framing, exact-root validation, unknown-attribute validation,\n cycle detection, scope push/pop, rightmost duplicate binding, per-frame fact\n index reset, and memo keys using the complete frame-vector stack. Completion\n means shuffled input produces the same semantic graph and all bindings resolve\n to the specified pairs.\n \n ### D2 — Merkle records and storage adapter\n \n Implement node hashes, ordered child hashes, hash-consing, the\n `:yin.debruijn/*` records, and the d5 storage adapter. Completion means no\n binder names, bound occurrence names, tail flags, or source tempids enter\n identity, and tree/graph emission has one semantic graph and fingerprint.\n \n ### D3 — canonical encoder and fingerprint\n \n Implement the descriptor-based SHA-256 encoder and numeric/string rules,\n-including distinct signed zero and integral-double-to-int64 canonicalisation.\n+including distinct signed zero and disjoint int64 and double classes.\n Completion means CLJ, CLJS, and CLJD produce identical bytes and all canonical\n fixtures hash identically across hosts.\n \n ### D4 — stream adapter\n \n Implement blocked, transport-error, invalid input, pending output, root\n framing, adjacent graphs (including graphs that reuse temporary eids), and\n terminal diagnostic outcomes. End-of-stream with a partial graph must be\n covered on every host.\n \n ### D5 — pipeline and storage integration\n \n D5 delivers a standalone `yin.vm.pipeline/persist-compiled!` adapter for the\n post-emission boundary before projected persistence; no Yang caller is wired\n yet. Store named and projected segments separately; use only the Merkle\n fingerprint for dedupe. Do not alter named consumers or runtime layers.\n \n ### D6 — end condition\n \n All D0–D5 criteria pass; alpha-equivalent programs have identical semantic\n projected tuples and root hashes; non-equivalent fixtures remain distinct;\n all emitter types and stream outcomes are covered; and AST, walker, VM,\n linearizer, named storage, lease, and waitset diffs are unchanged.\n \n ## 8. Test matrix\n \n The focused suite must include:\n \n * renamed binders at one and multiple nesting levels;\n * nearest shadowing with `[frame-depth position]` results;\n * duplicate parameters `(fn [x x] x)` proving rightmost-wins;\n * free names preserved and changed free names changing the fingerprint;\n * changed literals, primitive keys, arity, branches, stream operations, and\n   continuation markers changing the fingerprint;\n * zero-, one-, and multi-parameter lambdas and parameter order;\n * every `:vm/*`, `:stream/*`, `:if`, application, and\n   `:dao.stream.apply/call` emitter case;\n * shuffled datom input and all three host encoders producing identical bytes;\n * the same term emitted as a tree and as a graph producing identical Merkle\n   nodes and root fingerprint;\n * shared nodes under equal and unequal lexical contexts;\n * ordered operand changes producing different hashes;\n * `:yin/tail?` present versus absent producing identical output;\n * maps and sets with different iteration order producing identical encoding;\n * `+0.0` and `-0.0` hashing differently;\n-* an integral-double literal hashing identically to its int64 counterpart on\n-  every host;\n+* an integral-double literal hashing differently from its int64 counterpart\n+  on JVM and Dart (a JS number cannot tell them apart; that stays in the\n+  JS adapter);\n * dangling refs, duplicate facts, cycles, missing/multiple roots, unknown\n   node types, unknown `:yin/*` attributes, retracts, unexpanded macros,\n   unsupported values, and partial frames at end producing diagnostics;\n * adjacent rooted graphs in one input stream that reuse emitter eids beginning\n   at `-16`;\n * numeric boundary, NaN, signed-zero, JS-number, and NFC-limit fixtures;\n * blocked, transport-error, invalid-answer, and pending-write paths;\n * the essay's `(fn [count] (+ count 1))` example and stable fingerprint;\n * unchanged named AST reconstruction, linearization, semantic execution,\n   lease, and waitset suites.\n \n ## 9. What must not change\n \n The `:yin/*` schema, emitter, `datoms->ast`, walker, VM protocols, semantic\n machine, `yin.vm.linearize`, `:yin.code/*`, bijective named storage,\n `dao.stream`, transports, `dao.lease`, and `dao.stream.waitset` remain outside\n this design's authority. The only new pressure is a published projected\n dimension and optional diagnostic side index; neither changes the named form.\n \n ## 10. Resolved owner decisions\n \n The six open questions are resolved: source-leftmost parameter position is\n zero; duplicate names are rightmost-wins; shared nodes may be duplicated by\n lexical context but sharing is invisible to identity; maps and sets use\n canonical byte ordering; the published `:yin.debruijn/*` dimension supplies\n the domain separator and SHA-256 is the hash; the source-to-projected index is\n ephemeral by default and separate if later persisted. The Dart NFC source is\n `unorm_dart` (Unicode 16.0), settled by the owner 2026-09-21 and validated by\n the D3 cross-host fixtures. At the D0 review 2026-09-21 the value table's\n sequence class was ruled split: lists and vectors are distinct classes and\n never merge, because a program can observe the difference and identity never\n merges distinguishable values.\ndiff --git a/public/chp/blog/yin-vm-vs-unison.blog b/public/chp/blog/yin-vm-vs-unison.blog\nindex 8ddb10d4..0bfba49d 100644\n--- a/public/chp/blog/yin-vm-vs-unison.blog\n+++ b/public/chp/blog/yin-vm-vs-unison.blog\n@@ -1,96 +1,96 @@\n #:blog{:title \"Named Source, Canonical Identity: yin.vm and Unison's Different Trade-offs\"\n        :date #inst \"2026-09-21T22:26:54.000+07:00\"\n        :abstract [:p \"A look at the architectural trade-offs between Unison and yin.vm. Both derive code identity from a nameless, De Bruijn-style representation, but they diverge on where identity sits, whether moving a computation is an ability or an axiom, and whether a type is a gate or a fact. This post separates what yin.vm implements today from what is designed but not yet built.\"]\n        :content [:section.blog-article\n                  [:div.section-inner\n                   [:article\n                    :$blog-title\n                    :$blog-date\n \n                    [:p \"Unison and \" [:code \"datom.world\"] \" (via its \" [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" execution engine) both reject text files as the stored form of code. Unison identifies each definition by a hash of its syntax tree (\" [:a {:href \"https://www.unison-lang.org/docs/the-big-idea/\"} \"Unison documentation: the big idea\"] \"). \" [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" stores code as datoms (relational facts) and derives hash-based identities from them.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"Both systems use \" [:strong \"De Bruijn indexing\"] \" (representing variable bindings by position rather than by name) to make that identity independent of variable names. In Unison's hashing algorithm, variable names are converted to their De Bruijn index (\" [:a {:href \"https://unison-lang.org/whats-new/writeup-of-our-first-unison-meetup\"} \"Unison meetup writeup\"] \"). In yin.vm, executable images are derived from the named AST, address local variables by De Bruijn position, and are hashed without binder names. Where the two systems part ways is what is stored, how many identities one program has, what happens to a running computation, and what a type is.\"]\n \n                    [:h2 \"Status: What Exists Today\"]\n                    [:p \"Revised 2026-09-23. Each claim about \" [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" falls into one of three groups:\"]\n                    [:ul\n                     [:li [:strong \"Implemented: \"] \"the named Universal AST as datoms; its lowering to \" [:code \":yin.code/*\"] \" code that the semantic VM runs; a merged De Bruijn projection with Merkle fingerprints, stored as datoms and tested on JVM, JavaScript, and Dart; the De Bruijn \" [:em \"stack\"] \" path through B4 (the executable image and its hash \" [:code \"H\"] \", the named-datom resolver, the stack lowerer, the \" [:code \"yin.vm.debruijn.stack\"] \" VM, and its effects and engine seam); and the De Bruijn \" [:em \"register\"] \" path through R2 (a peer executable image with its own hash \" [:code \"R\"] \", its lowerer, validator, and effect contract).\"]\n                     [:li [:strong \"Designed, not yet implemented: \"] \"the register kernel (R4); the stream linker that fetches an image by \" [:code \"H\"] \" or \" [:code \"R\"] \" through \" [:code \"dao.jing\"] \" over \" [:code \"dao.stream\"] \" (B6, R5); runtime confinement of continuations under capability tokens (\" [:code \"docs/design/dao.space.security.md\"] \"); and a typecheck observer (\" [:code \"docs/design/yin.vm.typecheck.md\"] \").\"]\n                     [:li [:strong \"Later: \"] \"dependency closure for free names and the name-environment ledger (B7), a universal cross-model continuation format, and global distribution.\"]]\n                    [:p \"Statements about Unison cite Unison's documentation and its codebase schema, linked inline. Exact interoperability with Unison is not a goal of yin.vm; Unison is prior art.\"]\n \n                    [:h2 \"1. Unison: Hashing a Nameless Syntax Tree\"]\n                    [:p \"In a purely content-addressed system, code is identified by its cryptographic hash. But if you include variable names in that hash, you have a problem. If Developer A writes \" [:code \"(fn [timeout] (+ timeout 1))\"] \" and Developer B writes \" [:code \"(fn [retry] (+ retry 1))\"] \", a naive hashing algorithm sees two completely different functions, breaking deduplication and caching.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"Unison's hashing algorithm avoids this. Variable names are converted to their De Bruijn index, so name choices do not affect the hash: \" [:code \"id x = x\"] \" hashes the same as \" [:code \"identity a = a\"] \", because both \" [:code \"x\"] \" and \" [:code \"a\"] \" have De Bruijn index 0 (\" [:a {:href \"https://unison-lang.org/whats-new/writeup-of-our-first-unison-meetup\"} \"Unison meetup writeup\"] \"). Dependencies are replaced by their hashes (\" [:a {:href \"https://www.unison-lang.org/docs/the-big-idea/\"} \"Unison documentation: the big idea\"] \").\"]\n \n                    [:p \"Names are kept outside the hash. Unison's documentation describes them as \\\"separately stored metadata that don't affect the function's hash\\\", and states that the definition associated with a hash never changes when names are reassigned (\" [:a {:href \"https://www.unison-lang.org/docs/the-big-idea/\"} \"the big idea\"] \", \" [:a {:href \"https://www.unison-lang.org/docs/usage-topics/general-faqs/\"} \"general FAQ\"] \"). A rename therefore updates the name-to-hash mapping, and no definition changes.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"Read as a systems design, this is a \" [:strong \"distributed linker keyed by content hash\"] \". The hash is a location-independent linker symbol; two machines holding the same hash hold the same code by construction; fetching a definition from a peer is a hash lookup with no name resolution against the receiver's environment. Unison's \\\"arbitrary computations can just be moved from one location to another\\\" is a consequence of that linker, not a separate distribution feature.\"]\n \n                    [:h2 \"2. Where Human Names Live\"]\n                    [:p \"In Unison, the mapping of names to hashes is \\\"just metadata for humans, used to resolve names to hashes at parse time when slurping up code into the codebase, and used for the other direction when displaying code for the user\\\" (\" [:a {:href \"https://unison-lang.org/whats-new/writeup-of-our-first-unison-meetup\"} \"Unison meetup writeup\"] \"). The stored definition is identified by its hash, and its name is a separate mapping.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"In yin.vm, the names a developer wrote are part of the Named Universal AST itself, stored as datoms beside the structure they name. Every nameless form is derived from that named source, never the other way around.\"]\n \n                    [:h2 \"3. One Truth, Many Interpretations\"]\n                    [:p [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" is built on an architectural axiom: \" [:em \"Interpretation Creates Semantics (one truth, many perspectives).\"] \" Rather than one monolithic representation, it keeps one source of truth and derives other forms from it, each with its own identity or none:\"]\n                    [:ul\n                     [:li [:strong \"The Named Universal AST (implemented): \"] \"the canonical stored, queryable, and renderable form, with the exact names the developer wrote. It has no single hash. The AST walker evaluates it directly, and the semantic VM executes a separately linearized \" [:code \":yin.code/*\"] \" image lowered from it.\"]\n-                    [:li [:strong \"The De Bruijn projection and its fingerprint (implemented, dormant on the sharing path): \"] \"an alpha-canonical Merkle encoding of the named AST. Node hashes are hash-consed over a published dimension, transaction and provenance never enter the preimage, scalars are NFC-canonicalized, and the bytes are pinned identical on JVM, JavaScript, and Dart. Its records are stored as datoms. It is lossy by design: it folds \" [:code \"1.0\"] \" into \" [:code \"1\"] \" and drops tail flags, which makes it a good identity for alpha-equivalence and unusable as an executable form. This is the closest thing yin.vm has to Unison's hash: a hash of the nameless term.\"]\n+                    [:li [:strong \"The De Bruijn projection and its fingerprint (implemented, dormant on the sharing path): \"] \"an alpha-canonical Merkle encoding of the named AST. Node hashes are hash-consed over a published dimension, transaction and provenance never enter the preimage, scalars are NFC-canonicalized, and the bytes are pinned identical on JVM, JavaScript, and Dart. Its records are stored as datoms. Contract Version 2 preserves numeric type: \" [:code \"1.0\"] \" and \" [:code \"1\"] \" project to distinct records. It still drops tail flags, which makes it a good identity for alpha-equivalence and unusable as an executable form. This is the closest thing yin.vm has to Unison's hash: a hash of the nameless term.\"]\n                     [:li [:strong \"The resolved tuples (implemented, no identity): \"] \"the De Bruijn form of the named datoms that both executable lowerers consume. They carry exact scalars, free names, and tail flags, and they deliberately have no hash: they are a stage value, computed and discarded, under the project's \\\"derive, don't persist\\\" rule.\"]\n                     [:li [:strong \"The stack image, \" [:code \"H\"] \" (implemented): \"] \"a linear instruction vector with \" [:code \":load-bound [depth position]\"] \" for bound variables, \" [:code \":load-free name\"] \" for free ones, and arities instead of parameter names. \" [:code \"H\"] \" hashes the descriptor and the canonical vector; binder names live in a side table outside it.\"]\n                     [:li [:strong \"The register image, \" [:code \"R\"] \" (implemented through R2): \"] \"a peer of the stack image, lowered from the same resolved tuples, never from the stack image. \" [:code \"R\"] \" hashes its own descriptor and canonical register vector, including in-band live-register sets at every continuation boundary. Neither \" [:code \"H\"] \" nor \" [:code \"R\"] \" mentions the other.\"]]\n \n                    [:h3 \"3.1 One hash, or several?\"]\n                    [:p \"This is the first real divergence. Unison has \" [:strong \"one\"] \" hash per definition, and that hash does two jobs: it is the semantic identity of the term and it is the thing you fetch and run. Unison can afford that because it ships one runtime representation.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"yin.vm separates the jobs. The projection fingerprint identifies an alpha-equivalence class and is deliberately coarse. \" [:code \"H\"] \" and \" [:code \"R\"] \" identify exact executable bytes for one execution model each, and the design claims no law between them: equal resolved tuples give equal \" [:code \"H\"] \" and equal \" [:code \"R\"] \", but nothing says two programs with equal \" [:code \"H\"] \" have equal \" [:code \"R\"] \". That is why the same-root pairing of an \" [:code \"H\"] \" with an \" [:code \"R\"] \" is recorded as ordinary datoms beside the named root rather than inside either preimage, and why a receiver that wants proof re-lowers the named datoms and checks both hashes rather than comparing fingerprints. The named datoms are the one artifact every hash is a function of.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"The reason for paying that cost is the axiom above, applied one level down. The named VM, the stack VM, and the register VM are peer witnesses to one truth, bound only by agreement on observable behavior under a normalizer, never on internal mechanism. The owner accepted the permanent cost of a second evaluator (every opcode implemented and tested twice, on three hosts) because a running second kernel is the end-to-end proof that the \" [:code \"dao.stream\"] \" compilation pipeline has genuinely swappable backends. Unison never had to make this choice, so its single hash is simpler; yin.vm's layering is the price of a claim Unison does not make.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"When a developer renames a bound local variable, the named AST changes and neither \" [:code \"H\"] \" nor \" [:code \"R\"] \" nor the fingerprint moves. Renaming a free or global reference is different: free names stay inside the images as operands, so that change alters both. Dependency-by-hash indirection for free names, which is what gives Unison its rename behavior for global references, is phase B7 and is not built.\"]\n \n                    [:h2 \"4. Storage and Querying\"]\n                    [:p \"Unison's codebase is a SQLite database. Its schema stores terms, type declarations, and namespaces as binary blobs in an \" [:code \"object\"] \" table, keyed by hash, and adds indexes for type-based search (\" [:code \"find_type_index\"] \"), type mentions (\" [:code \"find_type_mentions_index\"] \"), dependents in both directions (\" [:code \"dependents_index\"] \"), and names (\" [:code \"scoped_term_name_lookup\"] \", \" [:code \"scoped_type_name_lookup\"] \") (\" [:a {:href \"https://github.com/unisonweb/unison/blob/trunk/codebase2/codebase-sqlite/sql/create.sql\"} \"codebase schema\"] \"). Unison's documentation describes the codebase as a database with \\\"perfect knowledge of dependencies\\\" and \\\"indices for type-based search\\\" (\" [:a {:href \"https://www.unison-lang.org/docs/the-big-idea/\"} \"the big idea\"] \"). The schema defines no index over the internal structure of a term's body, which is stored as opaque bytes.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"In \" [:code \"datom.world\"] \", the Named AST and the projection's hashed records are both stored as decomposed relational facts, so every node and edge is an independent tuple that can be indexed and queried with Datalog through \" [:code \"dao.space.query/q\"] \". Unison can tell you which definitions depend on a hash; \" [:code \"dao.space\"] \" can tell you which nodes inside a definition apply which operator to which operand, and it can do so over a program that is still growing, because the index is an incremental observer of the same stream the evaluator reads.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"The storage layer underneath is deliberately dumb in both systems. Unison's object table holds bytes keyed by hash. \" [:code \"dao.jing\"] \" is a passive, payload-agnostic key-value well with multihash content addressing that never indexes or interprets what it holds. All meaning lives in the interpreters that read from it.\"]\n \n                    [:h2 \"5. References and Distribution\"]\n                    [:p \"Unison replaces a definition's dependencies with their hashes (\" [:a {:href \"https://www.unison-lang.org/docs/the-big-idea/\"} \"the big idea\"] \"), so identity extends across the dependency graph. The \" [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" images keep free references by name, as \" [:code \":load-free\"] \" operands, and do not rewrite them to dependency hashes until B7.\"]\n \n                    [:h3 \"Sharing Code Over dao.stream (designed)\"]\n                    [:p \"The linker discipline is \" [:code \"dao.jing\"] \" over \" [:code \"dao.stream\"] \": one linker function, parameterized by a format record (\" [:code \"{:format ... :hash-fn ... :validate-fn ...}\"] \"), with \" [:code \"H\"] \" and \" [:code \"R\"] \" as its first two formats. Host B knows only a hash. It emits a request naming the format and the hash on a stream; the image or a qualified absence comes back on a stream. B verifies the value against its \" [:code \"dao.jing\"] \" address, recomputes the format hash from the received bytes, runs the format's validator (for register images, including the live-set rules), runs the free-name closure check, and only then loads it.\"]\n                    [:ul\n                     [:li [:strong \"The linker is a process between streams. \"] \"The VM never invokes a loader. Missing code parks the continuation and emits a request. Whether the peer is local or remote is the stream's concern, not the linker's; local versus remote is not a design axis.\"]\n                     [:li [:strong \"Integrity comes from the hash. \"] \"The receiver verifies content against \" [:code \"H\"] \" or \" [:code \"R\"] \" before loading. The one relation the linker cannot verify by hash is the same-root pairing between them; a receiver that follows that pairing without re-lowering is executing under composition trust and must say so in its outcome.\"]\n                     [:li [:strong \"Closed images come first. \"] \"B6 and R5 cover images with no free names. Dependency closure, including mutually recursive definitions hashed as a unit, is B7.\"]]\n \n                    [:p \"Where Unison folds \\\"resolve this hash, locally or from a peer\\\" into its codebase manager, yin.vm makes the seam itself an invariant: everything above \" [:code \"dao.stream\"] \" is forbidden from knowing whether a hash resolved from disk or from the network. Unison gets the same practical effect; it does not name the seam.\"]\n \n                    [:h2 \"6. Moving Computation: an Ability or an Axiom\"]\n                    [:p \"Unison's big-idea page says arbitrary computations can be moved between locations. In that sentence \\\"computation\\\" means hash-addressed code, which is trivially relocatable because its dependencies are already hashes. Moving a \" [:em \"running\"] \" computation -- a paused continuation with its captured state -- is a separate, opt-in capability in Unison, routed through an ability that appears in a function's type. A function whose type does not request it cannot migrate, and the compiler enforces that statically, at zero runtime cost.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"In \" [:code \"datom.world\"] \" migration is not a capability; it is axiom 4: \" [:em \"everything is a continuation\"] \", serializable, portable, able to pause, travel across streams, and resume. A continuation is datoms from the start, so it is simultaneously a resumable machine state to an evaluator and queryable facts to \" [:code \"dao.space\"] \". The register design makes this concrete: a saved continuation is a sparse payload carrying only the registers a verified live set proves can still be read, plus the image identity that parked it, and a restore whose VM model or image does not match refuses with a qualified outcome rather than interpreting a foreign payload. Continuation transport (implemented as pure payload constructors and validators; the kernel that runs them is R4) is a defined protocol, not ambient access.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"The trade is explicit. Generalizing migration to every computation removes Unison's ability to say, in a type, \" [:em \"this one must never leave\"] \". The project's answer is not a type; it is \" [:strong \"confinement\"] \". \" [:code \"docs/design/dao.space.security.md\"] \" commits the unit of sharing to a governed \" [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" AST rather than data, and splits safety in two: \" [:em \"execution safety\"] \" (a capability token, macaroon-style, scopes the continuation's environment and store, exposes no I/O, and bounds it by steps) and \" [:em \"data safety\"] \" (the AST is restricted to a bounded-output sub-language in which returning the rows verbatim is not expressible, checked syntactically by Datalog over the AST itself). Unison's ability system addresses the first; it has no analogue of the second, because an ability-checked function may still return whatever it touched. That design is a target: the same document records that runtime confinement is not yet realized and that the sub-language is an open problem. Today yin.vm enforces less than Unison here while aiming at more.\"]\n \n                    [:h2 \"7. Types: Gates or Facts\"]\n                    [:p \"Unison's types and abilities are gates. An ill-typed term is never hashed into the codebase; a term whose effects are unhandled is refused. The value of the static layer is that refusal, and Unison obtains it by making the checker, the codebase, and the hash one system.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"In \" [:code \"datom.world\"] \" a type is a fact beside code: a datom on an occurrence, with a provenance slot saying who asserted it -- the front end from a source ascription, an inference process, a trace observer of a run, or a checker. Static and dynamic typing differ in the provenance of the type facts, not in the kind of code (see \" [:a {:href \"/blog/yin-vm-ast-chinese-characters.blog\"} \"Chinese Characters for Programming Languages\"] \"). That is the representational half, and it subsumes Unison's: a Unison signature is one such fact. The operational half is an observer. \" [:code \"docs/design/yin.vm.typecheck.md\"] \" specifies a typecheck process on the syntax side of a medium boundary, upstream of every evaluator, exactly where the macro expander sits. It reads a program medium and the claim rows, runs one pure checker, appends a verdict citing its evidence, and forwards the program unchanged or not at all. A refused program never lands on the medium evaluators read, so it never acquires an \" [:code \"H\"] \" or \" [:code \"R\"] \". Evaluators know nothing about types, and type facts never enter any hash preimage.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"Two things follow that Unison's arrangement cannot offer. First, there is no mode, only topology: attach the evaluators' cursors to the unchecked medium and the observer is advisory; attach them to the checked one and it is a gate; leave it out and the program is dynamic. Unison is the gate row with a fixed checker. Second, because every effect a program performs is already a row on a stream, a declared effect set and an observed one are two fact sets over the same program, and their disagreement is a query. Unison checks a term once when it is added; this design can keep checking a running program. None of it is built; T0 to T2 are specified and no type language is chosen.\"]\n \n                    [:h2 \"8. Can Each Implement the Other?\"]\n                    [:p \"Both are Turing-complete, so the question is what is native and enforced versus simulated by discipline. Every piece of Unison's model has a place in \" [:code \"datom.world\"] \" without touching an evaluator: terms as named datoms and their De Bruijn projection, one hash as the fingerprint, the hash-keyed store as \" [:code \"dao.jing\"] \", fetch-by-hash as the stream linker, abilities as effect descriptors already on a stream plus a typecheck observer, handlers capturing continuations as axiom 4, and migration as continuation transport. Unison's model is, from this side, a particular configuration of observers. In the other direction, Unison can host a CESK interpreter over datoms, a tuple space, and a Datalog engine as a program, but its own runtime would still have one privileged evaluator, callbacks at the host boundary, and one hash on the term. It can implement \" [:code \"datom.world\"] \"'s semantics as an application; it cannot adopt its invariants without becoming a different system.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"So \" [:code \"datom.world\"] \" is the more general model, and Unison is the more complete implementation. Unison's static checks exist and run; yin.vm's typecheck observer, confinement, and dependency closure are specified. What Unison does not have is a queryable term structure, a second peer executable identity, or a place to put the difference between a declared effect and an observed one.\"]\n \n                    [:h2 \"Conclusion\"]\n                    [:p \"Unison hashes a nameless form of each definition, keeps names as separate metadata, and uses that one hash both as identity and as the thing it fetches and runs. \" [:code \"yin.vm\"] \" keeps the names in the source AST, derives a coarse semantic fingerprint and two exact peer executable identities from it, and treats the sharing of those images over \" [:code \"dao.stream\"] \" as the point of the exercise.\"]\n \n                    [:p \"Today yin.vm has the named source, the semantic VM, the projection and fingerprint, the De Bruijn stack VM through B4, and the register path through R2. The register kernel, the stream linker, confinement, and the typecheck observer are designed and not yet built. Dependency closure and global distribution come after that. Where the two systems agree -- code as a nameless, hash-identified graph -- they agree closely. Where they differ, the difference is always the same one: Unison builds a guarantee into one system; \" [:code \"datom.world\"] \" turns it into an observer on a stream and pays for the generality in phases it has not finished.\"]\n                    ]]]}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
